      \field{abstract}{In both human and wildlife disease systems, temporal shifts in host immunity may shape the timing and severity of epidemics. Yet, immune responses, as well as seasonal patterns in their expression, are difficult to measure. Rather, field studies collect phenomenological data on infection outcomes. Pairing epidemic data of multiple outbreaks with models that directly parameterize immune metrics can be a powerful approach for exploring the role of time-varying immunity on disease. Field data can be used to determine how well a parameterized model can reproduce trends and differences observed among outbreaks.}
